WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.758
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.058 --> 00:00:08.735
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:16.029
Unit ten: Crisis Management. Today you will contrast setbacks and outcomes with even after / despite clauses.

4
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:18.958
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.156
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:25.122
Crisis.

7
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:29.475
Crisis means a time of intense difficulty or danger.

8
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:33.859
The factory fire became a reputational crisis within hours.

9
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.056
Your turn. Say: Crisis.

10
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.878
Crisis.

11
00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:43.117
Contingency plan.

12
00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:48.055
Contingency plan means a plan prepared for a possible emergency.

13
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:52.577
The airline's contingency plan rerouted passengers overnight.

14
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:56.161
Your turn. Say: Contingency plan.

15
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.442
Contingency plan.

16
00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:02.660
Response protocol.

17
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:08.076
Response protocol means a predefined sequence of actions during an emergency.

18
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:11.902
The hospital's response protocol activated within minutes.

19
00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:15.599
Your turn. Say: Response protocol.

20
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.864
Response protocol.

21
00:01:20.617 --> 00:01:21.966
Transparency.

22
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.225
Transparency means openness about facts, even unwelcome ones.

23
00:01:27.817 --> 00:01:31.035
Transparency about the error prevented rumours from spreading.

24
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.523
Your turn. Say: Transparency.

25
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:38.774
Transparency.

26
00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:40.773
Damage control.

27
00:01:41.783 --> 00:01:45.362
Damage control means actions taken to limit harm after a problem.

28
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.774
Damage control began before the press conference.

29
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:52.256
Your turn. Say: Damage control.

30
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.484
Damage control.

31
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.808
Stakeholder trust.

32
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:04.568
Stakeholder trust means the confidence that interested groups place in an organisation.

33
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:07.462
Stakeholder trust took years to rebuild.

34
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.027
Your turn. Say: Stakeholder trust.

35
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.077
Stakeholder trust.

36
00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:16.945
Escalation.

37
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.749
Escalation means a rapid increase in seriousness or intensity.

38
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.929
Silence from management led to an escalation of protests.

39
00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:29.111
Your turn. Say: Escalation.

40
00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:33.200
Escalation.

41
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:34.951
Recovery.

42
00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.563
Recovery means the process of returning to a normal or better state.

43
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.951
The brand's recovery depended on visible reforms.

44
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.202
Your turn. Say: Recovery.

45
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.033
Recovery.

46
00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:54.635
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.483 --> 00:03:01.851
Visal, a food company discovered contamination in one batch of fish sauce. Should it stay silent to avoid panic?

48
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:11.756
Absolutely not. Even after a contamination scare, trust can be preserved if the company acts quickly and openly.

49
00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.687
But a recall costs millions.

50
00:03:15.783 --> 00:03:29.012
So does silence, eventually. Despite the short-term cost, a voluntary recall signals integrity, whereas concealment risks escalation if journalists discover the truth.

51
00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:32.035
What should the first statement say?

52
00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:43.395
What happened, what's being done and how customers can protect themselves. Even after mistakes, people forgive honesty far more readily than evasion.

53
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:46.068
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:50.158
Grammar focus: Even after and despite clauses.

55
00:03:50.458 --> 00:03:51.657
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.580
Trust can be preserved even after a difficult crisis.

57
00:03:56.483 --> 00:04:00.399
Despite the short-term cost, a recall signals integrity.

58
00:04:01.283 --> 00:04:04.978
Even after admitting the error, the company kept its customers.

59
00:04:05.933 --> 00:04:15.366
'Even after' stresses that a result holds despite a later stage of difficulty; 'despite' (no 'of') takes a noun or -ing form. Do not write 'despite of' or 'despite the company lost'.

60
00:04:16.117 --> 00:04:20.255
Be careful! Don't say: Even after the crisis happened it.

61
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.117
Say: Even after the crisis, trust remained.

62
00:04:25.417 --> 00:04:27.440
Even after + noun / clause.

63
00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:31.153
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:33.154
The Golden Hour.

65
00:04:33.454 --> 00:04:42.001
Crisis-management specialists often refer to a 'golden hour', the brief period in which an organisation's first response shapes public perception.

66
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:50.712
Even after serious failures, companies that communicate promptly and transparently tend to recover faster than those that delay or deny.

67
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:59.586
The classic example is Johnson & Johnson's 1982 Tylenol recall, which, despite enormous cost, ultimately strengthened the brand.

68
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:07.162
Effective response protocols assign clear roles in advance, preventing confusion and contradictory statements.

69
00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:14.717
Recovery, however, requires more than words: stakeholders must see concrete reforms that make a repeat of the crisis unlikely.

70
00:05:15.833 --> 00:05:19.603
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.285
blank the delay, the shipment arrived intact.

72
00:05:23.585 --> 00:05:27.332
A, Despite. B, Despite of. C, Even though.

73
00:05:30.867 --> 00:05:32.971
The answer is A: Despite.

74
00:05:34.117 --> 00:05:37.440
A plan prepared for a possible emergency is a blank.

75
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.438
A, contingency plan. B, recovery. C, escalation.

76
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.510
The answer is A: contingency plan.

77
00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.068
A rapid increase in seriousness is blank.

78
00:05:52.368 --> 00:05:57.019
A, damage control. B, transparency. C, escalation.

79
00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:02.891
The answer is C: escalation.

80
00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.678
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:05.978 --> 00:06:15.042
A, Despite the company lost money, it recovered.. B, Despite losing money, the company recovered.. C, Despite of losses, the company recovered..

82
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.515
The answer is B: Despite losing money, the company recovered..

83
00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.752
According to the reading, the Tylenol recall ultimately…

84
00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:32.622
A, destroyed the brand. B, strengthened the brand. C, was hidden from the public.

85
00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:38.886
The answer is B: strengthened the brand.

86
00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.414
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.364
Why is transparency important during a crisis?

88
00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:05.557
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:05.857 --> 00:07:42.799
Transparency is important because, during a crisis, information gaps are quickly filled by rumour and suspicion. When an organisation explains what happened, what it is doing and what people should do, it regains a measure of control over the narrative. Even after serious mistakes, stakeholders tend to forgive candour, whereas concealment, once exposed, transforms an accident into a scandal. For example, a prompt product recall may be costly, but it demonstrates that public safety outweighs profit.

90
00:07:43.099 --> 00:07:44.901
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:45.817 --> 00:07:51.197
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and even after and despite clauses.

92
00:07:51.497 --> 00:07:56.216
Today's value is resilience. Prepare for setbacks, and recover from them with strength.

93
00:07:56.516 --> 00:07:58.925
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:59.225 --> 00:08:02.388
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

